Key takeaways
Runtime policy enforcement shifts enterprise AI governance from retrospective compliance audits into active, inline risk prevention. AI-powered exposure management unifies security visibility by tracking interconnected risks across workforce browser extensions, hosted foundation models, and connected databases. Session-scoped ephemeral credentials paired with runtime execution guardrails restrict agent blast radius and prevent unauthorized tool executions from r...



&nbsp;

Executive Summary&nbsp;

AI governance is cloud governance. It must go beyond inventory and policies to prove that AI systems operate securely across IaaS, PaaS and SaaS. Connecting discovery, posture assessment, adversarial testing, runtime monitoring and remediation provides evidence of what AI can access, how it behaves, and whether security controls work.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Key Takeaways&nbsp;


	AI inventory must extend beyond cloud APIs.&nbsp;
